Scott 1972, Lemma 3.9. Let e embed X as a subspace of Y, and let a projection between continuous lattices D and D' have retraction j and section i. If continuous maps f from X to D and g from X to D' satisfy f = j ∘ g, then the maximal extension of f equals j composed with the maximal extension of g.

The extensions of f and of g are continuous, and so are j composed with the extension of g and i composed with the extension of f, once the Scott topologies are registered locally. The composite of j with the extension of g solves the extension problem for f: on the image of the embedding it reduces, by the continuity clause for the extension of g, to j ∘ g, which is f. Maximality of the extension of f therefore places this composite below that extension. The composite of i with the extension of f lies below g on the image of the embedding: the extension of f recovers f, the hypothesis rewrites f as j ∘ g, and i ∘ j lies below the identity. The subsolution form of maximality therefore places this composite below the extension of g. The retraction-section identity writes the extension of f as j ∘ i of itself, monotonicity of j and the second bound place that below j of the extension of g, and the first bound is the opposite inequality.
